: "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Illinois remains a Democratic-leaning state in presidential elections, with a partisan lean of roughly six points toward Democrats. The 2028 race is fully open because the incumbent president is term-limited, and no general-election polls or expert ratings are available yet, so the forecast rests on the state's fundamentals. Recent headlines center on Governor JB Pritzker's possible 2028 run and Democratic wins in the 2026 midterms, neither of which points to a fundamental shift in the state's lean.\n\nDemocrats have carried Illinois comfortably in recent cycles, though the margin narrowed in 2024 compared with 2020. A home-state Pritzker nomination would likely widen the Democratic margin, while a strong Republican national environment could tighten it. With polling thin and the field unsettled, uncertainty is wider than usual, and the race rates as a Democratic lean rather than anything stronger.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7608, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.746, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7069, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.7035, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.7139, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.7208, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.7158, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.714, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.7198, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.727, "margin": 6.0}]}